{"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"major":0,"patch":0},"sections":[],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/using-imported-c-macros-in-swift"},"seeAlsoSections":[{"anchor":"Language-interoperability","title":"Language interoperability","generated":true,"identifiers":["doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/c-interoperability","doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/cxx-interoperability","doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/customizing-your-c-code-for-swift","doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/using-imported-c-structs-and-unions-in-swift","doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/using-imported-c-functions-in-swift"]}],"abstract":[{"text":"Use imported C-defined macros as constants.","type":"text"}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Overview","type":"heading","anchor":"Overview","level":2},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Swift automatically imports simple, constant-like macros, declared with the ","type":"text"},{"code":"#define","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"directive, as global constants. Macros are imported when they use literals for string,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"floating-point, or integer values, or use operators like ","type":"text"},{"code":"+","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"-","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":">","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", and ","type":"text"},{"code":"==","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"between literals or previously defined macros. This example defines some simple macros","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"in a C header:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"occ","code":["#define FADE_ANIMATION_DURATION 0.35","#define VERSION_STRING \"2.2.10.0a\"","#define MAX_RESOLUTION 1268","","#define HALF_RESOLUTION (MAX_RESOLUTION \/ 2)","#define IS_HIGH_RES (MAX_RESOLUTION > 1024)"],"type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"When imported into Swift, the macros in the above example are equivalent to these","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"constant declarations:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","code":["let FADE_ANIMATION_DURATION = 0.35","let VERSION_STRING = \"2.2.10.0a\"","let MAX_RESOLUTION = 1268","","let HALF_RESOLUTION = 634","let IS_HIGH_RES = true"],"type":"codeListing"},{"level":3,"text":"Use Functions and Generics Instead of Complex Macros","anchor":"Use-Functions-and-Generics-Instead-of-Complex-Macros","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"C macros that are more complex than simple constant definitions have no counterpart","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"in Swift. You use complex macros in C and Objective-C to avoid type-checking constraints","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"or to avoid retyping large amounts of boilerplate code. However, macros can make","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"debugging and refactoring difficult. In Swift, you can use functions and generics","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"to achieve the same results without any compromises.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/swift\/using-imported-c-macros-in-swift"]}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift"]]},"metadata":{"title":"Using Imported C Macros in Swift","role":"article","modules":[{"name":"Standard Library"}],"roleHeading":"Article"},"kind":"article","references":{"doc://com.apple.Swift/documentation/Swift/c-interoperability":{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Use imported C types or call C variadic functions."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/c-interoperability","type":"topic","role":"collectionGroup","title":"C Interoperability","kind":"article","url":"\/documentation\/swift\/c-inte